#!/usr/bin/env node
// Second Key — MCP server (stdio, zero dependencies).
//
// Exposes the Second Key protocol as two native tools any MCP client can
// hold:
//   screen_message        — judge an inbound message/request
//                           (PROCEED / VERIFY / STOP + matched patterns)
//   consequence_statement — restate a planned action in plain language,
//                           with the verification checklist
//
// The protocol logic is the same engine the hosted API runs
// (functions/api/_engine.js + _patterns.js). This file is generated by
// mcp/build-mcp.mjs, which inlines those two modules so the server is a
// single file an agent can install with nothing but Node 18+.
//
// Config (optional):
//   SECOND_KEY_API  — if set to the hosted base URL, calls go to the API
//                     instead of the local engine (same answers; the API
//                     adds the paid/eval tiers). Default: local, offline.

const API_BASE = process.env.SECOND_KEY_API || null;

// ==== BEGIN INLINED PROTOCOL CORE (from _patterns.js + _engine.js) ====
// Second Key — elder-fraud pattern library (data).
//
// Each pattern: id, name (plain), whatItIs (one plain sentence for output),
// action (the ladder step when this pattern fires on its own: "stop" fires
// only with a hard signal present; see _engine.js), and signals.
// Each signal: id, label (plain language, shown to the user), re (RegExp),
// points, hard (true = this signal alone can push a matched pattern to STOP).
//
// Hard signals are the classic loss mechanics: pay by gift card / wire /
// crypto on demand, secrecy from family, threats of arrest, fees to claim a
// prize, requests for passwords / codes / account numbers, remote access to
// the computer, "move the money to a safe account".

